{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Come, let us deal wisely with him.</b> There is good wisdom and there is evil wisdom. Wisdom in Torah and in ethics is good wisdom, while wisdom in matters of cunning is evil wisdom. And this 'dealing wisely' (התחכמות) is not wisdom, for it did not say 'Come, let us be wise' (הבה נחכמה); and Scripture says (Ecclesiastes 7:16): ״אל תתחכם יותר״ — it warns against this התחכמות which belongs to this [evil] kind of wisdom, for it did not say אל תחכם יותר. And this התחכמות is that whereby the Egyptians came upon them with stratagems: first in their property, as it is written וישימו עליו שרי מסים — he imposed a heavy tax upon them, and he would show them that this was not out of enmity, for it is the way of the kings of the earth to levy taxes upon their subjects. Afterward he decreed enslavement upon their bodies, as it is written ויעבידו מצרים את בני ישראל בפרך — Pharaoh declared Israel abandoned property, so that each and every Egyptian had permission to take any Israelite to perform his labor; this is what it says בפרך. And this 'perach' is that they would make them bring the earth and prepare the clay and the bricks and build the building, and they would give them nothing but straw. And after that he added to the decree and withheld straw from them, as it says (Exodus 5:18): ותבן לא ינתן לכם ותכן לבנים תתנו. And after that he added to the decree and embittered their lives with heavy yoke, more repugnant than all the above, as it is written: וימררו את חייהם בעבודה קשה בחומר ובלבנים ובכל עבודה בשדה — that is, besides the forced labor (פרך) mentioned in the earlier verse, which is 'hard labor in clay and bricks,' they further embittered their lives with 'all labor in the field.' And the labor in the field is the digging of ditches in their fields and the removal of dung; this is what it says את כל עבודתם — that is, besides their labor which they subjected them to בפרך." "<b>And behold,</b> Pharaoh the wicked intended, with all these forms of affliction, to wear down the strength of Israel and to diminish their seed so that they would not be fruitful and multiply — for exertion wears down a person's strength and diminishes seed. And since he saw that they were not diminished through all this but kept multiplying, as it is written וכאשר יענו אותו כן ירבה, therefore he decreed killing upon them. And know that had he wished to order them killed all at once, it would have been possible for him; but because it would be an act of great treachery for a king to kill the people of his land who came to dwell in his country under his pledge of faith, and this would be a disgrace to the king among the nations, therefore he did not wish to kill them publicly, but sought to outwit them to destroy them in secret and stealthily, and so that the violence done to them would not be known. Therefore he commanded the matter to the two women, the midwives, that they kill the males on the birth-stool, as it is written: ויאמר מלך מצרים למילדות וגו׳ אם בן הוא והמתן אותו. And he commanded them to conceal the matter and not reveal it to any person, so that the matter would be a secret from everyone — needless to say from the Egyptians, whom he would fear more, but even from Israel, and even from the mothers who gave birth themselves — for it is possible for a midwife to claim to the mother that the child was born dead. And when Pharaoh saw that the fear of HaShem, blessed be He, had prevailed over the midwives more than the fear of the king, and they kept the males alive, and this stratagem had availed him nothing, he had to reveal the matter to the Egyptians, as it is written ויצו פרעה לכל עמו וגו׳. And still Israel were entirely unaware of it, for when an Israelite father of the child would come to complain to the king or to the judge, they would say to him: Bring witnesses and I will avenge you. And after that the matter became public to the whole world, and even to Israel, and the decree was open to all the Egyptians: they would enter the houses by royal command and search for the children at night, lest their mothers hide them, as it is written ולא יכלה עוד הצפינו וגו׳. Scripture teaches you that each and every woman of the women of Israel — the months of her pregnancy were known to the Egyptian officials investigating on behalf of the government, for they would investigate everything and nothing was hidden from the king — and they would come when her days were full to give birth, so that even if she wanted to hide her son, she had no permission." "<b>And behold,</b> all this cunning which they practiced in secret and in public availed them nothing, and the blessing coursed into their seed with great multiplication like the growth of the field, as it is written וכאשר יענו אותו כן ירבה וכן יפרוץ. And so the prophet said (Ezekiel 16:7): רבבה כצמח השדה נתתיך ותרבי ותגדלי. And even though they cast their children into the Nile, behold — the deliverer was saved through a cause from HaShem, blessed be He, for deliverance and success belong to Him and all causes are from Him. And He, blessed be He, frustrated their counsel and destroyed their wisdom, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 44:25): משיב חכמים אחור ודעתם יסכל. And likewise it shall befall us in this exile of ours: in every generation they devise evil against us and scheme to destroy us from the world, but they have no power, for such are not the thoughts of HaShem, blessed be He. As our Sages of blessed memory expounded: וכאשר יענו אותו כן ירבה — the Holy One, blessed be He, said: You say פן ירבה, but I say כן ירבה." "<b>And they said</b> in Midrash Tehillim (Psalm 9): למנצח על מות לבן — the enemy has finished swords forever. Philipus asked Rabbi El'asha: Is it not so that the prophet said (Malachi 1:4) כי תאמר אדום רוששנו ונשוב ונבנה חרבות כה אמר ה' אלהים המה יבנו ואני אהרוס — so everything we have built stands! He said to him: The verse speaks not of buildings but of counsels — all the time that you sit year by year and scheme to destroy us, He demolishes your counsel. He said to him: By your life, so it is: we sit every single year to destroy you, and one cause from Heaven comes and nullifies it for us. And just as the Egyptians dealt evil to Israel — first in their property and afterward in their bodies — so the Holy One, blessed be He, measured against them measure for measure, and exacted retribution from them first in their property, as it is written (Exodus 9:6): וימת כל מקנה מצרים; and afterward He exacted retribution from their bodies, as it says (ibid.): כי היה השחין בחרטמים ובכל מצרים; and after that He exacted retribution from them with death, as it says (Exodus 12:30): כי אין בית אשר אין שם מת." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> הבה נתחכמה לו — let us deal wisely with Him, that is, with the King of the universe. If we burn them — they are the children of Abraham, who was saved from the fiery furnace. If we slay them with the sword — they are the children of Isaac, who was saved from the sword. If we stone them with stones — they are the children of Jacob, who was elevated through a stone, as it is written (Genesis 49:24): משם רעה אבן ישראל, and furthermore their kingdom is likened to a stone, as it is written: ואבנא די מחת לצלמא הות לטור רב ומלאת כל ארעא. Rather — let us cast them into the water, for He has sworn not to bring a flood upon the world. Therefore He drowned them in water, as it is written (Exodus 18:11): כי בדבר אשר זדו עליהם — in the very broth they cooked, they were cooked. And so the prophet said (Isaiah 66:4): גם אני אבחר בתעלוליהם ומגורותם אביא להם." "<b>And let him go up from the land.</b> Scripture used a figurative expression, for it should have said 'and let us go up.'" "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> ״ועלה מן הארץ״ — as long as Israel is in the lower descent, they ascend; this is ״ועלה מן הארץ״ — from within the descent there is ascent. And so David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 44:26): ״דבקה לארץ בטננו״, and immediately after it is written: ״קומה עזרתה לנו״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {4 "The citation וימת כל מקנה מצרים is Exodus 9:6; the printed reference reads (שמות ט׳:ו׳) meaning Exodus 9:6. The subsequent citation כי אין בית אשר אין שם מת is Exodus 12:30; the printed (שם יב) appears to abbreviate the chapter number of the same book."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:36.435058283Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}